---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="The Redacted Rabbis — Daf Yomi Game: Chullin 85"
  description="Chullin 85: the Mishnah says 'the Rabbis' twice — but it's hiding two different sages behind the same word. Read the evidence, unmask the redacted Tanna, catch each one's real exception, and help Rabbi Chiyya outwit the moths in his flax."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#0a0d10] !text-[#e7ecef]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#232b33] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#12171c]/80 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#d1a44c]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#e8c476]">daf yomi arcade</a> · chullin 85 · 2026-07-24
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">🕵️ The Redacted Rabbis</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl !text-[#d1a44c]" dir="rtl" lang="he">וּשְׁנָאוֹ בִּלְשׁוֹן חֲכָמִים</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-1 text-xs italic !text-[#8b98a3]">"...and he taught it in the language of the Rabbis" — Chullin 85a:9</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#b7c0c7]">
        Two mishnayot in this tractate rule on the same dispute — whether an invalid act of slaughter still
        counts as "slaughter." Both times the Mishnah writes the anonymous plural
        <strong class="!text-[#e7ecef]">"the Rabbis."</strong> Both times the Gemara reveals that Rebbi, the
        redactor, tucked one individual sage's opinion behind that word — and
        <strong class="!text-[#e7ecef]">it isn't the same sage twice.</strong> Open the case files. Read the
        evidence. Name the name.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Context card ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#12171c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d1a44c]">before you open the file</p>
        <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c476]">🔪 shechita she'eina re'uya</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#8b98a3]">
              "Slaughter that isn't fit" — cutting an animal's throat properly, but for a reason that makes the
              meat forbidden anyway (it's a <em>tereifa</em>, it was meant for idol worship, etc). The whole
              question: does that still count as an act of "slaughter" for other halakhot?
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c476]">🩸 kisui hadam</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#8b98a3]">
              The mitzva to cover the blood of a slaughtered undomesticated animal or bird (Leviticus 17:13).
              Chullin 85's own mishna asks: does it apply after an unfit slaughter?
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c476]">🐐 oto v'et beno</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#8b98a3]">
              "It and its offspring you shall not slaughter in one day" (Leviticus 22:28). Chullin 81b asks the
              identical question about the same phrase: unfit shechita — slaughter or not?
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3">
            <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c476]">🖋️ "the Rabbis"</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#8b98a3]">
              When a mishna rules anonymously in the name of "the Rabbis," it usually means a majority. Rebbi's
              trick here: sometimes it's cover for a single Tanna's view he happened to like better (85a:9).
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Dossier / score bar ============ -->
      <div class="sticky top-0 z-10 mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-1 rounded-xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#0a0d10]/95 px-4 py-2.5 text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] backdrop-blur">
        <span class="!text-[#8b98a3]">dossier:</span>
        <span id="score-unmasked" class="!text-[#d1a44c]">unmasked 0/2</span>
        <span id="score-loophole" class="!text-[#d1a44c]">loopholes 0/2</span>
        <span id="score-moth" class="!text-[#d1a44c]">moth case: open</span>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Case File 1: oto v'et beno ============ -->
      <div id="case-1" class="case-card mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#171d24] p-5" data-correct="meir">
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
          <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d1a44c]">case file 01 · Chullin 81b:4</p>
          <span class="stamp">OPEN</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#b7c0c7]">
          One slaughters an animal, then its offspring, on the same day — but the first slaughter turns out to
          be invalid (it's found to be a <em>tereifa</em>, or was done for idol worship, or was the red heifer,
          or a beast condemned to be stoned). Does the prohibition of <em>oto v'et beno</em> still apply to the
          second animal?
        </p>
        <div class="mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3">
          <p class="text-xs !text-[#8b98a3]">
            <strong class="!text-[#e7ecef]">Rabbi Shimon</strong> rules <strong class="!text-[#e8c476]">exempt</strong>
            — an unfit slaughter isn't "slaughter," so the prohibition never triggered.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs !text-[#8b98a3]">
            <strong class="redact-name" data-answer="Rabbi Meir">▓ REDACTED ▓</strong> — hiding behind
            <strong class="!text-[#e7ecef]">"the Rabbis"</strong> — rules
            <strong class="!text-[#e8c476]">liable</strong>. Who is it?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <button class="evidence-btn" data-target="case-1-clue-a">🔍 Evidence A — the source verse</button>
          <button class="evidence-btn" data-target="case-1-clue-b">🔍 Evidence B — the parallel case</button>
        </div>
        <div id="case-1-clue-a" class="clue hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#3a2f18]/60 bg-[#1b160a] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#c9b98a]">
          The redacted sage reads a <strong>gezeirah shavah</strong> — the shared word "slaughter" links two
          verses: <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">לֹא תִשְׁחֲטוּ</span> (Leviticus 22:28, <em>oto v'et
          beno</em>) and <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">אוֹ אֲשֶׁר יִשָּׁחֵט</span> (Leviticus 17:3,
          offerings slaughtered outside the Temple).
        </div>
        <div id="case-1-clue-b" class="clue hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#3a2f18]/60 bg-[#1b160a] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#c9b98a]">
          An offering slaughtered outside the Temple is never fit to permit its meat for eating — yet the Torah
          still calls that act "slaughter" and punishes it as such. If unfit slaughter counts as slaughter
          <em>there</em>, the redacted sage reasons, it counts here too (Chullin 85a:10–11).
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button class="accuse-btn" data-sage="meir">Accuse Rabbi Meir</button>
          <button class="accuse-btn" data-sage="shimon">Accuse Rabbi Shimon</button>
        </div>
        <div id="case-1-verdict" class="verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Case File 2: kisui hadam ============ -->
      <div id="case-2" class="case-card mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#171d24] p-5" data-correct="shimon">
        <div class="mb-3 flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
          <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d1a44c]">case file 02 · Chullin 85a:7 (this daf's own mishna)</p>
          <span class="stamp">OPEN</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#b7c0c7]">
          Same fact pattern, different mitzva: one slaughters an undomesticated animal or bird and it turns out
          to be invalid (<em>tereifa</em>, idol worship, <em>chullin</em> in the Temple courtyard, or something
          condemned to be stoned). Is one obligated to cover its blood?
        </p>
        <div class="mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3">
          <p class="text-xs !text-[#8b98a3]">
            <strong class="!text-[#e7ecef]">Rabbi Meir</strong> rules <strong class="!text-[#e8c476]">obligated</strong>
            — named right in the mishna this time.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs !text-[#8b98a3]">
            <strong class="redact-name" data-answer="Rabbi Shimon">▓ REDACTED ▓</strong> — hiding behind
            <strong class="!text-[#e7ecef]">"the Rabbis"</strong> — rules
            <strong class="!text-[#e8c476]">exempt</strong>. Same two suspects. Who is it this time?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <button class="evidence-btn" data-target="case-2-clue-a">🔍 Evidence A — the source verse</button>
          <button class="evidence-btn" data-target="case-2-clue-b">🔍 Evidence B — the exclusion</button>
        </div>
        <div id="case-2-clue-a" class="clue hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#3a2f18]/60 bg-[#1b160a] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#c9b98a]">
          The redacted sage borrows from Joseph's steward: <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">טְבוֹחַ טֶבַח
          וְהָכֵן</span>, "slaughter an animal and prepare it" (Genesis 43:16) — a slaughter meant to feed
          people. Only a slaughter <em>fit to be eaten</em> counts as "slaughter" for this mitzva
          (Chullin 85a:12).
        </div>
        <div id="case-2-clue-b" class="clue hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border border-[#3a2f18]/60 bg-[#1b160a] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#c9b98a]">
          The kisui hadam verse itself specifies an undomesticated animal or bird
          <span class="frank" dir="rtl" lang="he">אֲשֶׁר יֵאָכֵל</span> — "that may be eaten" (Leviticus
          17:13). Rabbi Meir reads that phrase as excluding only a ritually impure bird; the redacted sage reads
          it as excluding any unfit slaughter — including a <em>tereifa</em> (Chullin 85a:19).
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <button class="accuse-btn" data-sage="meir">Accuse Rabbi Meir</button>
          <button class="accuse-btn" data-sage="shimon">Accuse Rabbi Shimon</button>
        </div>
        <div id="case-2-verdict" class="verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-xl border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Loophole test ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#171d24] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d1a44c]">the loophole test — Rabbi Abba, Chullin 85b:1</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#8b98a3]">
          Neither sage is consistent across every halakha. Rabbi Abba names one crack in each position. True or
          false?
        </p>

        <div id="loophole-1" class="loophole-card mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3" data-correct="false">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#e7ecef]">"Rabbi Meir says an unfit slaughter counts as slaughter for
            <em>every</em> halakha it touches."</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2">
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="true">True</button>
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="false">False</button>
          </div>
          <div class="loophole-verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">
            <strong>False.</strong> He concedes an unfit slaughter never renders the meat permitted to eat. Case
            in point: a <em>tereifa</em> is slaughtered and a living nine-month-old fetus is found inside — the
            mother's invalid slaughter does not exempt the fetus from needing its own slaughter
            (Chullin 85b:3, 85b:5–6).
            <a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85b.3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chullin 85b:3 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="loophole-2" class="loophole-card mt-4 rounded-xl border border-[#1c232a] bg-[#0e1216] p-3" data-correct="false">
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#e7ecef]">"Rabbi Shimon says an unfit slaughter is <em>never</em> considered
            an act of slaughter, for any halakha."</p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex gap-2">
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="true">True</button>
            <button class="tf-btn" data-value="false">False</button>
          </div>
          <div class="loophole-verdict hidden mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">
            <strong>False.</strong> He concedes it still purifies the animal from the impurity of an
            unslaughtered carcass. The Torah's extra word in "and if <em>any</em> of the animals should die"
            (Leviticus 11:39) teaches that some slaughter transmits carcass-impurity and some doesn't — and a
            slaughtered <em>tereifa</em> is the "doesn't" (Chullin 85b:7).
            <a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85b.7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chullin 85b:7 →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Bonus: Rabbi Chiyya's moths ============ -->
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#171d24] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d1a44c]">bonus case — Rabbi Chiyya's moths, Chullin 85b:12–16</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#8b98a3]">
          Moths infested Rabbi Chiyya's flax. Rebbi's fix: slaughter a bird over the tub where the flax was
          soaked — the moths smell the blood and flee. One problem: a baraita says that if you slaughter a bird
          and you actually need the blood for something else, you're <em>still</em> obligated to cover it and
          forbidden to reuse it. So how did Rabbi Chiyya get away with it? Two sages, both arriving from Eretz
          Yisrael, report two different things Rebbi told him. Tap each option below.
        </p>
        <div id="moth-grid" class="mt-4 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2"></div>
        <p id="moth-summary" class="mt-3 hidden text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#c9b98a]">
          Both real answers avoid the problem the same way the mishna does: if it's never a valid <em>shechita</em>,
          there's no obligation to cover the blood at all (85a:8). Rav Dimi and Ravin just disagree about which
          route Rebbi actually named — and the Gemara isn't finished asking why he didn't pick the simpler one.
          That question spills over into tomorrow's daf, 86a.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-[#232b33] bg-[#12171c] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#d1a44c]">learn the real daf</p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#b7c0c7]">
          Chullin 85 opens by finishing what 84b started: Rabbi Yosei's attempt to prove, from the shofar blown
          by an intersex <em>tumtum</em>, that covering a <em>koy</em>'s blood overrides a Festival. Ravina and
          Rabbi Elazar HaKappar the Distinguished each refute the comparison — first from circumcision, then by
          noting circumcision never applies on Festival nights at all — and Rabbi Abba confirms this is one of
          the arguments Rabbi Chiyya says has no comeback
          (<a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85a.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85a:1–6</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#b7c0c7]">
          Then the mishna this game is built from: four cases of unfit slaughter, with Rabbi Meir and "the
          Rabbis" split on covering the blood, plus universal agreement that a botched slaughter, a stabbing, or
          a torn-out windpipe never obligates covering at all
          (<a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85a.7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85a:7–8</a>).
          The Gemara then unmasks both this mishna and the parallel one at 81b as Rebbi's editorial handiwork —
          each anonymous ruling is really one Tanna's opinion, traced back through gezeirot shavot to the verses
          of offerings slaughtered outside the Temple, Joseph's steward, and the phrase "that may be eaten"
          (<a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85a.9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85a:9–20</a>).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#b7c0c7]">
          Amud b opens with Rabbi Abba's two carve-outs — the <em>ben tesha chai</em> fetus case and the
          carcass-purity concession — then a detour into whether slaughtering non-sacred animals in the Temple
          courtyard is forbidden by Torah or rabbinic law
          (<a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85b.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85b:1–11</a>).
          The daf closes on Rabbi Chiyya's moth-eaten flax and the two conflicting reports of Rebbi's advice —
          an argument the Gemara carries, unresolved, into 86a
          (<a class="!text-[#e8c476] underline hover:!text-[#f2d9a3]" href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85b.12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85b:12–16</a>).
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Chullin.85a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#d1a44c]/40 bg-[#d1a44c]/10 px-3 py-1.5 text-xs font-bold !text-[#e8c476] hover:bg-[#d1a44c]/20">Chullin 85 on Sefaria →</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rashi_on_Chullin.85a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#232b33] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8b98a3] hover:!text-[#e7ecef]">Rashi (×42)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Steinsaltz_on_Chullin.85a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#232b33] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8b98a3] hover:!text-[#e7ecef]">Steinsaltz (×37)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Ritva_on_Chullin.85a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#232b33] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8b98a3] hover:!text-[#e7ecef]">Ritva (×15)</a>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Rabbeinu_Gershom_on_Chullin.85a" class="rounded-lg border border-[#232b33] px-3 py-1.5 text-xs !text-[#8b98a3] hover:!text-[#e7ecef]">Rabbeinu Gershom (×14)</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style>
    .frank {
      font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    }
    .stamp {
      border: 2px solid #8f4038;
      color: #e2645f;
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 900;
      letter-spacing: 0.15em;
      padding: 2px 8px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      transform: rotate(-3deg);
      text-transform: uppercase;
    }
    .stamp.closed {
      border-color: #6f8f4a;
      color: #a7d17a;
    }
    .redact-name {
      display: inline-block;
      background: #05070a;
      color: #05070a;
      border-radius: 3px;
      padding: 0 6px;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
      transition: background-color 0.4s ease, color 0.4s ease;
    }
    .redact-name.revealed {
      background: transparent;
      color: #e8c476 !important;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .evidence-btn {
      border: 1px solid #3a2f18;
      background: #1b160a;
      color: #c9b98a;
      font-size: 11px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 10px;
      text-align: left;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .evidence-btn:hover { border-color: #d1a44c; color: #e8c476; }
    .evidence-btn.seen { border-color: #6f8f4a; color: #a7d17a; }
    .clue.hidden { display: none; }
    .accuse-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a3742;
      background: #0e1216;
      color: #e7ecef;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 8px 14px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .accuse-btn:hover { border-color: #d1a44c; color: #e8c476; }
    .accuse-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.55; }
    .case-card.solved .accuse-btn { opacity: 0.4; }
    .case-card.solved .accuse-btn.chosen { opacity: 1; border-color: #d1a44c; color: #e8c476; }
    .verdict.hidden { display: none; }
    .verdict.correct { border-color: #6f8f4a; background: #10160c; color: #cfe3ac; }
    .tf-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a3742;
      background: #0e1216;
      color: #e7ecef;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 700;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 6px 16px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .tf-btn:hover { border-color: #d1a44c; color: #e8c476; }
    .tf-btn:disabled { cursor: default; opacity: 0.5; }
    .loophole-verdict.hidden { display: none; }
    .loophole-verdict { border-color: #6f8f4a; background: #10160c; color: #cfe3ac; }
    .moth-btn {
      border: 1px solid #2a3742;
      background: #0e1216;
      color: #e7ecef;
      font-size: 12px;
      font-weight: 600;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 10px;
      text-align: left;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    .moth-btn:hover { border-color: #d1a44c; }
    .moth-btn.right { border-color: #6f8f4a; background: #10160c; }
    .moth-btn.wrong { border-color: #8f4038; background: #180d0b; }
    .moth-explain { margin-top: 6px; font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.5; color: #a7b0b8; }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .fade-in { animation: fadeIn 0.35s ease both; }
      @keyframes fadeIn { from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); } to { opacity: 1; transform: none; } }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id);
    const $$ = (sel: string, root: ParentNode = document) => Array.from(root.querySelectorAll(sel));

    // ==================================================================
    // Case files — Chullin 81b:4 (oto v'et beno) and Chullin 85a:7 (kisui
    // hadam). Both mishnayot rule anonymously in the name of "the Rabbis";
    // the Gemara at 85a:9 reveals each "Rabbis" is really one Tanna's view
    // that Rebbi found more convincing and folded into the plural.
    // ==================================================================
    let unmaskedCount = 0;
    let loopholeCount = 0;

    function initCase(caseId: string) {
      const card = $(caseId);
      if (!card) return;
      const correct = card.getAttribute('data-correct');

      $$('.evidence-btn', card).forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const target = btn.getAttribute('data-target');
          if (!target) return;
          const clue = $(target);
          if (clue) {
            clue.classList.remove('hidden');
            clue.classList.add('fade-in');
          }
          btn.classList.add('seen');
        });
      });

      $$('.accuse-btn', card).forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (card.classList.contains('solved')) return;
          const guess = btn.getAttribute('data-sage');
          const verdictEl = $(caseId + '-verdict');
          const nameEl = card.querySelector('.redact-name') as HTMLElement | null;
          const stampEl = card.querySelector('.stamp') as HTMLElement | null;

          card.classList.add('solved');
          btn.classList.add('chosen');

          if (nameEl) {
            nameEl.textContent = nameEl.getAttribute('data-answer') || '';
            nameEl.classList.add('revealed');
          }
          if (stampEl) {
            stampEl.textContent = 'CLOSED';
            stampEl.classList.add('closed');
          }

          if (verdictEl) {
            verdictEl.classList.remove('hidden');
            verdictEl.classList.add('correct', 'fade-in');
            const sageName = correct === 'meir' ? 'Rabbi Meir' : 'Rabbi Shimon';
            const wasRight = guess === correct;
            verdictEl.innerHTML = wasRight
              ? `<strong>Correct.</strong> Rebbi tucked ${sageName}'s ruling behind "the Rabbis" here (Chullin 85a:9).`
              : `<strong>Not quite</strong> — it was ${sageName} hiding behind "the Rabbis" (Chullin 85a:9). The name is unmasked above either way.`;
          }

          unmaskedCount += 1;
          const scoreEl = $('score-unmasked');
          if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `unmasked ${unmaskedCount}/2`;
        });
      });
    }

    initCase('case-1');
    initCase('case-2');

    // ==================================================================
    // Loophole test — Rabbi Abba's two concessions, Chullin 85b:1.
    // ==================================================================
    $$('.loophole-card').forEach((card) => {
      const correct = card.getAttribute('data-correct');
      let answered = false;
      $$('.tf-btn', card).forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (answered) return;
          answered = true;
          const val = btn.getAttribute('data-value');
          const verdict = card.querySelector('.loophole-verdict');
          $$('.tf-btn', card).forEach((b) => (b as HTMLButtonElement).disabled = true);
          if (val === correct) {
            btn.classList.add('right' as never);
            (btn as HTMLElement).style.borderColor = '#6f8f4a';
            (btn as HTMLElement).style.color = '#a7d17a';
          } else {
            (btn as HTMLElement).style.borderColor = '#8f4038';
            (btn as HTMLElement).style.color = '#e2645f';
          }
          if (verdict) {
            verdict.classList.remove('hidden');
            verdict.classList.add('fade-in');
          }
          loopholeCount += 1;
          const scoreEl = $('score-loophole');
          if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = `loopholes ${loopholeCount}/2`;
        });
      });
    });

    // ==================================================================
    // Bonus — Rabbi Chiyya's moths, Chullin 85b:12–16. Two authentic
    // amoraic traditions (Rav Dimi / Ravin) report two different things
    // Rebbi told him; both routes independently work because both land
    // in the mishna's own exempt categories (85a:7–8).
    // ==================================================================
    const MOTH_OPTIONS = [
      {
        id: 'a',
        label: 'Slaughter it properly, then take the blood for the flax.',
        result: 'wrong',
        explain: 'Wrong. A baraita says: one who slaughters and needs the blood for something else is still obligated to cover it, and may not divert it (Chullin 85b:13).',
      },
      {
        id: 'b',
        label: 'Tear out the windpipe and gullet instead of slaughtering.',
        result: 'right',
        explain: 'Right — Ravin\'s report: Rebbi said "go tear it" (85b:14). Since it isn\'t a valid shechita, there\'s no kisui hadam obligation at all (matches the mishna\'s own exemption, 85a:8).',
      },
      {
        id: 'c',
        label: 'Render it a tereifa first, then slaughter it.',
        result: 'right',
        explain: 'Right — Rav Dimi\'s report: Rebbi said "go make it a tereifa" (85b:14). A slaughtered tereifa is exempt from kisui hadam per this very mishna (85a:7).',
      },
      {
        id: 'd',
        label: 'Ignore it — moths aren\'t a halakhic problem.',
        result: 'wrong',
        explain: 'Wrong — Rebbi himself gave Rabbi Chiyya the blood-and-moths remedy (85b:12); the only question is how to do it without an obligation to cover.',
      },
    ];

    const mothGrid = $('moth-grid');
    let mothRightFound = 0;
    if (mothGrid) {
      MOTH_OPTIONS.forEach((opt) => {
        const wrap = document.createElement('div');
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'moth-btn w-full';
        btn.textContent = opt.label;
        const explain = document.createElement('div');
        explain.className = 'moth-explain hidden';
        explain.textContent = opt.explain;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const already = explain.classList.contains('revealed-once');
          explain.classList.remove('hidden');
          explain.classList.add('fade-in');
          btn.classList.add(opt.result === 'right' ? 'right' : 'wrong');
          btn.disabled = true;
          if (opt.result === 'right' && !already) {
            explain.classList.add('revealed-once');
            mothRightFound += 1;
            if (mothRightFound >= 2) {
              const summary = $('moth-summary');
              const scoreEl = $('score-moth');
              if (summary) { summary.classList.remove('hidden'); summary.classList.add('fade-in'); }
              if (scoreEl) scoreEl.textContent = 'moth case: closed';
            }
          }
        });
        wrap.appendChild(btn);
        wrap.appendChild(explain);
        mothGrid.appendChild(wrap);
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
